<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/avaricum"><span class="enc-name">Avaricum</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Avaricum was the chief oppidum of the Bituriges Cubi in Gaul, on the site of modern Bourges.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/avaris"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/avaris.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Avaris</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Avaris was an ancient city in the north-eastern Nile Delta, at the modern site of Tell el-Dab'a.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/avars"><span class="enc-name">Avars</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Avars were a nomadic people who established a powerful khaganate in the Pannonian basin, roughly present-day Hungary, from the sixth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/avarua"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/avarua.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Avarua</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Avarua is the capital of the Cook Islands, on the north coast of Rarotonga.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aventicum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aventicum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aventicum</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Aventicum was the capital of the Helvetii under Roman rule, on the site of the modern town of Avenches in western Switzerland, near Lake Murten.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aventine-hill"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aventine-hill.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aventine Hill</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aventine is the southernmost of the seven hills of Rome, beside the Tiber.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/avenue-of-the-dead"><span class="enc-name">Avenue of the Dead</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Avenue of the Dead is the main axis of Teotihuacan in central Mexico, a broad ceremonial way more than two kilometres long laid out in the early centuries CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/averroes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/averroes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Averroes</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Averroes, or Ibn Rushd, was an Andalusian philosopher, physician and judge of the twelfth century who served the Almohad caliphs.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aveyron"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aveyron.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aveyron</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Aveyron is a department in southern France, in the Occitanie region, covering most of the former province of Rouergue.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/avgustyn-voloshyn"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/avgustyn-voloshyn.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Avgustyn Voloshyn</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Avgustyn Voloshyn was a Greek Catholic priest, teacher and Ukrainian political leader in Subcarpathian Ruthenia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/avicenna"><span class="enc-name">Avicenna</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Avicenna, Ibn Sina, was a Persian philosopher and physician born near Bukhara under the Samanids.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/avidius-cassius"><span class="enc-name">Avidius Cassius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Avidius Cassius was a Roman general born in Syria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/avienus"><span class="enc-name">Avienus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Avienus (Rufius Festus Avienius) was a Latin poet of the 4th century CE who adapted Greek works on geography and astronomy into verse.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/avignon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/avignon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Avignon</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Avignon is a city on the Rhône in Provence, southern France, the Avennio of the Cavares and of Roman Gaul.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/avila"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/avila.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ávila</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ávila is a city in Castile and León, Spain, standing at more than 1,100 metres on the northern Meseta west of Madrid.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/avitus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/avitus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Avitus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Avitus was a Gallo-Roman aristocrat who became Western Roman emperor in 455 with the backing of the Visigothic king Theodoric II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/avongara"><span class="enc-name">Avongara</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Avongara are the aristocratic ruling clan of the Zande peoples of Central Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/avranches"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/avranches.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Avranches</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Avranches is a town in the Manche department of Normandy, north-western France, overlooking the bay of Mont-Saint-Michel.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/avranchin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/avranchin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Avranchin</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Avranchin is the historic district around Avranches in south-western Normandy, France, facing Mont-Saint-Michel across the bay.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/avungara"><span class="enc-name">Avungara</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Avungara are the ruling aristocratic clan of the Zande people of north-central Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/avvakum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/avvakum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Avvakum</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Avvakum Petrov was a Russian archpriest and leader of the Old Believers who opposed the liturgical reforms of Patriarch Nikon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awa-odori"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/awa-odori.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awa Odori</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">Awa Odori is a summer dance festival held in Tokushima, on the Japanese island of Shikoku, during the Obon season in August.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awa-province-tokushima"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/awa-province-tokushima.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awa Province (Tokushima)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Awa was an old province of Japan in the east of the island of Shikoku, corresponding to present-day Tokushima Prefecture.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awadh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/awadh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awadh</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Awadh, or Oudh, is a region of north-central Uttar Pradesh in India, between the Ganges and the Himalayan foothills.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awaji-island"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/awaji-island.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awaji Island</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Awaji Island is the largest island of Japan's Inland Sea, lying between Honshu and Shikoku and now part of Hyogo Prefecture.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awami-league"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/awami-league.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awami League</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Awami League is a political party of Bangladesh, founded in Dhaka in 1949 as a rival to the Muslim League in East Pakistan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awariku"><span class="enc-name">Awariku</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Awariku, called Urikki in Assyrian texts, was a king of Que (Hiyawa) on the Cilician plain in the 8th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awash-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/awash-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awash River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Awash River is a river in Ethiopia that flows from the central highlands towards the Afar lowlands.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awdaghast"><span class="enc-name">Awdaghast</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Awdaghast was a trading town in the southern Sahara, in present-day Mauritania, that served as a terminus of trans-Saharan caravan routes.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-awhad-ayyub"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/al-awhad-ayyub.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al-Awhad Ayyub</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Awhad Ayyub was an Ayyubid prince, son of al-Adil, who governed Mayyafariqin in Upper Mesopotamia for his father from about 1200.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awjila"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/awjila.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awjila</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Awjila is an oasis town in the Libyan Desert of eastern Libya, some 250 km inland from the Gulf of Sirte.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awlad-al-balad"><span class="enc-name">Awlad al-balad</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The awlad al-balad, meaning 'sons of the country', were the noble ruling elite of the Wadai Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awoamefia"><span class="enc-name">Awoamefia</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Awoamefia is the title of the paramount ruler of the Anlo Ewe of south-eastern Ghana, who resides at Anloga.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awole"><span class="enc-name">Awole</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Awole was an Alaafin of the Oyo Empire in the late eighteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awori"><span class="enc-name">Awori</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Awori are a Yoruba subgroup of the coastal lagoons of south-western Nigeria, in present-day Lagos and Ogun states.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awraba"><span class="enc-name">Awraba</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Awraba were a Berber tribal confederation of north-western Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awujale"><span class="enc-name">Awujale</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Awujale is the title of the paramount ruler of the Ijebu Kingdom, a Yoruba state in south-western Nigeria with its seat at Ijebu-Ode.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awwam-temple"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/awwam-temple.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awwam Temple</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Awwam Temple, known in Arabic as Mahram Bilqis, is the great sanctuary of the god Almaqah near Marib in Yemen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/axayacatl.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Axayacatl</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Axayacatl was the sixth tlatoani of Tenochtitlan, ruling in the later fifteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/axel-oxenstierna"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/axel-oxenstierna.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Axel Oxenstierna</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Axel Oxenstierna was Lord High Chancellor of Sweden from 1612 until his death in 1654.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/axim"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/axim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Axim</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Axim is a coastal town in the Western Region of Ghana.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers"><span class="enc-name">Axis powers</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Axis powers were the alliance of Germany, Italy and Japan, with other partners, that fought the Allies in the Second World War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ayacucho.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ayacucho</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayacucho is a city in the central highlands of southern Peru and the capital of the region of the same name.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayanis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ayanis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ayanis</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayanis is the site of a Urartian fortress on the eastern shore of Lake Van in eastern Turkey, founded by Rusa II in the seventh century BCE and known in antiquity as…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayarmaca"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ayarmaca.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ayarmaca</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ayarmaca were an Andean people whose lordship, centred north-west of Cusco in Peru, was one of the main rivals of the early Inca kingdom.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayas-cilicia"><span class="enc-name">Ayas (Cilicia)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayas, the Laiazzo of Italian merchants, is a port on the Gulf of İskenderun in southern Turkey, now the town of Yumurtalık.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayawaso"><span class="enc-name">Ayawaso</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayawaso was the inland capital of the Ga kingdom on the Accra plains of present-day Ghana.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayaz-kala"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ayaz-kala.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ayaz-Kala</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayaz-Kala is a group of fortresses on and around a hill at the edge of the Kyzylkum Desert in Karakalpakstan, north-western Uzbekistan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aybak"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aybak.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aybak</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aybak, or Izz al-Din Aybak, was a Mamluk commander who became the first sultan of the Mamluk Sultanate in Egypt in 1250.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aydinoglu-mehmed-bey"><span class="enc-name">Aydınoğlu Mehmed Bey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aydınoğlu Mehmed Bey was a commander in the service of the Germiyanids who founded the Aydinid beylik in western Anatolia around 1308.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayia-irini-kea"><span class="enc-name">Ayia Irini</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayia Irini is a Bronze Age settlement on a small peninsula in the bay of Agios Nikolaos on the island of Kea, in the Cyclades, Greece.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayia-triada"><span class="enc-name">Ayia Triada</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayia Triada is a Minoan archaeological site in southern Crete, about three kilometres west of the palace of Phaistos.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayios-vasileios"><span class="enc-name">Ayios Vasileios</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayios Vasileios is a Mycenaean palace site near Xirokambi, south of Sparta in Laconia, Greece.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aymara"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aymara.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aymara</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aymara are an Indigenous people of the Andean altiplano around Lake Titicaca, in Bolivia, Peru and Chile, speaking the Aymara language.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya"><span class="enc-name">Ayodhya</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayodhya is a city on the Sarayu River in Uttar Pradesh, India, revered by Hindus as the birthplace and capital of Rama in the Ramayana.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayrarat"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ayrarat.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ayrarat</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayrarat was a central province of historic Armenia, in the plain of the Aras river around Mount Ararat.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayrshire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ayrshire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ayrshire</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayrshire is a historic county in