ype">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayurbarwada Buyantu Khan, known by the temple name Renzong, was emperor of the Yuan dynasty from 1311 to 1320.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayushiridara"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ayushiridara.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ayushiridara</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayushiridara, known as Biligtü Khan, was the son of Toghon Temür, the last Yuan emperor of China, and ruled the Northern Yuan in Mongolia from 1370 to 1378.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ayutthaya Kingdom</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ayutthaya Kingdom was a Siamese state founded in 1351 in central Thailand.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayyanadikal-thiruvadikal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ayyanadikal-thiruvadikal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ayyanadikal Thiruvadikal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayyanadikal Thiruvadikal was the ruler of Venad in southern Kerala in the mid-ninth century, under the overlordship of the Chera Perumal king Sthanu Ravi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ayyubid Empire</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ayyubid Empire was a Sunni Muslim dynasty founded by Saladin, who ended the Fatimid Caliphate in Egypt in 1171 and united Egypt and Syria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/az-zahir-ghazi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/az-zahir-ghazi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Az-Zahir Ghazi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Az-Zahir Ghazi was a son of Saladin and Ayyubid ruler of Aleppo from 1186 to 1216.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azad-kashmir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azad-kashmir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azad Kashmir</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Azad Kashmir is a self-governing territory administered by Pakistan in the western part of the former princely state of Jammu and Kashmir, with its capital at…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/azad-khan-afghan"><span class="enc-name">Azad Khan Afghan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Azad Khan Afghan was an Afghan commander who rose to power in north-western Iran after the death of Nader Shah.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azai-hisamasa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azai-hisamasa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azai Hisamasa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Azai Hisamasa was a Japanese daimyo of northern Ōmi province, son of Azai Sukemasa and father of Azai Nagamasa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azai-nagamasa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azai-nagamasa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azai Nagamasa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Azai Nagamasa was a Japanese daimyo of northern Ōmi province who led the Azai clan from 1560.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azai-sukemasa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azai-sukemasa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azai Sukemasa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Azai Sukemasa was a Japanese warlord of the Sengoku period who founded the power of the Azai clan in northern Ōmi province.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azare"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azare.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azare</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Azare is a town in Bauchi State in northern Nigeria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/azariqa"><span class="enc-name">Azariqa</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Azariqa were the most militant faction of the Kharijites, named after their leader Nafi ibn al-Azraq, who broke away at Basra around 684.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azarmidokht"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azarmidokht.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azarmidokht</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Azarmidokht was a Sasanian queen who ruled briefly around 630–631, during a period of rapid succession after the death of Khosrow II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azawagh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azawagh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azawagh</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Azawagh is a dry basin and a fossil river valley in the southern Sahara and Sahel, mainly in north-western Niger with parts in Mali and Algeria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azaz"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azaz.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azaz</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Azaz is a town in north-western Syria, north of Aleppo near the Turkish border.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azcapotzalco</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Azcapotzalco was the capital of the Tepanec state in the Valley of Mexico, which dominated the region in the late fourteenth and early fifteenth centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azd"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azd.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azd</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Azd were a major group of Arab tribes originating in South Arabia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azekah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azekah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azekah</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Azekah was an ancient fortified town in the Shephelah, the low hills of Judah, at Tel Azekah in present-day Israel.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azemmour"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azemmour.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azemmour</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Azemmour is a town on the Oum Er-Rbia river near its mouth on the Atlantic coast of Morocco.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azerbaijan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azerbaijan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Azerbaijan is a region of the southern Caucasus and north-western Iran, centred historically on Tabriz.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azes-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azes-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azes I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Azes I was an Indo-Scythian king of north-western India in the 1st century BCE who ruled over Gandhara and the Punjab.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-azhar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/al-azhar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al-Azhar</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Azhar is a mosque in Cairo begun in 970 under the Fatimid Caliphate, soon after the founding of the city.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/azim-ud-din-i-of-sulu"><span class="enc-name">Azim ud-Din I of Sulu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Azim ud-Din I, also called Alimuddin I, was Sultan of Sulu in the eighteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aziru"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aziru.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aziru</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aziru was the ruler of Amurru in the fourteenth century BCE and the son of Abdi-Ashirta.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/al-aziz.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">al-Aziz</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Aziz is a name borne by two different rulers mentioned on the site.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz-billah"><span class="enc-name">al-Aziz Billah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Aziz Billah was the fifth Fatimid caliph and the first to begin his reign in Egypt, ruling from 975 to 996.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azizi-mosque"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azizi-mosque.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azizi Mosque</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Azizi Mosque is a mosque at Tanjung Pura in Langkat Regency, North Sumatra, Indonesia, built from 1899 to 1902 by Sultan Abdul Aziz of Langkat.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aznar-galindez-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aznar-galindez-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aznar Galíndez I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aznar Galíndez I was the first known count of Aragon, ruling the small county around Jaca in the central Pyrenees in the early 9th century under Frankish protection.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azores"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azores.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azores</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Azores are an archipelago of nine volcanic islands in the North Atlantic, about 1,400 kilometres west of mainland Portugal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azov"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azov.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azov</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Azov is a town in Rostov oblast, southern Russia, on the Don near the Sea of Azov.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aztec-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aztec Empire</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aztec Empire was the Triple Alliance of Tenochtitlan, Texcoco and Tlacopan, formed in 1428 in the Valley of Mexico and dominated by the Mexica of Tenochtitlan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aztec-purepecha-war-of-1479"><span class="enc-name">Aztec–Purépecha war of 1479</span><span class="enc-type">Campaign · 1479</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aztec–Purépecha war of 1479 was a military campaign by the Aztec ruler Axayacatl against the Purépecha (Tarascan) kingdom to the west.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aztlan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aztlan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aztlan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Aztlan is the legendary homeland from which the Mexica (Aztecs) and other Nahua peoples were said to have migrated to central Mexico.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azuay-province"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azuay-province.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azuay Province</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Azuay is a province in the southern highlands of Ecuador, with its capital at Cuenca.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/azul-ruben-dario"><span class="enc-name">Azul (Rubén Darío)</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Azul is a collection of short stories and poems by the Nicaraguan poet Rubén Darío, published in Valparaíso, Chile, in 1888.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/azzan-ibn-qais"><span class="enc-name">Azzan ibn Qais</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Azzan ibn Qais was a member of the Al Bu Said family who, backed by the Ibadi religious leaders of the Omani interior, took Muscat in 1868 and was elected imam.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azzo-vii-deste"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azzo-vii-deste.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azzo VII d'Este</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Azzo VII d'Este, called Novello, was marquis of Este and leader of the Guelph party in north-eastern Italy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/azzone-visconti"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/azzone-visconti.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Azzone Visconti</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Azzone Visconti was lord of Milan from 1329 to 1339.</span></a></li></ul>
